\chapter{Cache persistence and management}
\chapterprecis{Written by Dan-Claude and reviewed by Daniel Bosk.}

We want to persist the Canvas object between runs to avoid redundant API calls.
The Canvas object contains cached data for courses, assignments, users, and
submissions.
By saving this object to disk, we can reuse it on subsequent runs until the
cache expires or is explicitly cleared.

This persistence layer now serves two clients.
The command-line interface uses it when running subcommands, and the top-level
[[canvaslms]] package uses it to expose a lazy [[canvaslms.canvas]] object to
Python programs.
That package-level entry point also lets other command-line tools import
[[canvaslms]] and reuse the same configured, cached Canvas object.
That shared loader keeps both interfaces consistent: they restore the same
encrypted cache, configure the same retry policy, and fail in the same way when
credentials are missing.

However, the Canvas object contains sensitive data (course information, student
data, etc.), so we must encrypt it before storing to disk.
We use authenticated encryption to ensure both confidentiality and integrity.

Additionally, we cache submission attachments to avoid re-downloading files.
This is managed separately from the Canvas object cache.

\section{Design overview}

The cache persistence mechanism works as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
\item When creating a Canvas object, we first check if a cached version exists.
\item If it does, we decrypt and unpickle it using the Canvas token as the key.
\item If not, we create a new Canvas object from the API.
\item After each command completes, we save the Canvas object to the cache,
unless the command sent no request to Canvas, since then the file already
holds what we have (\cref{sec:skip-unchanged-save}).
\item The cache is stored in the user's cache directory, which we take from
the package-level [[dirs]] instance (\cref{sec:shared-appdirs}) so that this
cache and the attachment store share one directory.
\item We provide [[cache clear]] and [[cache clear-attachments]] subcommands.
\end{enumerate}

The key derivation works as follows:
We use the Canvas access token as the password for key derivation.
This means that if the user changes their token, the old cache becomes
inaccessible (which is correct behavior, as it's tied to a specific account).

A cache is only useful if it is right, and five mechanisms keep this one fresh
while keeping what it has.
\begin{description}
\item[Time to live] Each kind of listing expires after an interval suited to how
often it changes (\cref{sec:cache-ttl-constants}).
\item[Invalidation on write] A command that changes something in Canvas forgets
the listings it made wrong, for instance [[invalidate_quiz_listing_cache]].
\item[Refresh on a miss] A name that matches nothing makes the search look at
Canvas once more (\cref{sec:refresh-on-miss}).
\item[Refresh on request] [[--refresh-cache]] fetches again what the command reads
(\cref{sec:refresh-on-request}).
\item[Repair on read] A listing stored with a restriction, for instance by an
older version, is fetched properly the next time it is read
(\cref{sec:subset-listings}).
\end{description}
The third and fourth exist because the first two cannot know about a change made
elsewhere, such as being added to a course.
A request with a restriction skips all of this: it goes straight to Canvas and is
not stored.
All of them replace objects without losing what is cached below them
(\cref{sec:cache-copy-to-new}).
The time to live and the two refreshes add and update entries, and only a 404 on
the individual refresh of a stale object removes one, so an entry that Canvas no
longer lists stays in the cache.

