\subsection{Normalising the Canvas hostname}

Users naturally think in hostnames like [[canvas.kth.se]], but the
[[canvasapi]] client needs a full base URL.
We centralise this normalization so that the CLI and package API cannot drift.

<<functions>>=
def normalize_hostname(hostname):
  """Return ``hostname`` as a URL suitable for ``canvasapi.Canvas``."""
  if "://" not in hostname:
    hostname = f"https://{hostname}"
  return hostname
@


\subsection{Configuring retry policy with exponential backoff}

The shared loader is also the right place to configure retry behaviour.
Without this, the command-line client and the package API could end up with
different resilience characteristics even though they talk to the same Canvas
server.

The retry policy itself stays unchanged.
We still retry transient failures such as [[429]], [[500]], [[502]], [[503]],
and [[504]] using exponential backoff with jitter.
The only difference is where that policy lives: in the shared loader rather
than in the CLI bootstrap.

<<functions>>=
def configure_canvas_retry_policy(canvas):
  """Install the shared retry policy on a Canvas session."""
  retry_strategy = Retry(
    total=10,
    backoff_factor=2,
    backoff_max=256,
    backoff_jitter=0.3,
    status_forcelist=[429, 500, 502, 503, 504],
    allowed_methods=["HEAD", "GET", "PUT", "DELETE", "OPTIONS", "TRACE", "POST"],
    respect_retry_after_header=True,
  )

  adapter = HTTPAdapter(max_retries=retry_strategy)
  canvas._Canvas__requester._session.mount("https://", adapter)
  canvas._Canvas__requester._session.mount("http://", adapter)
  return canvas
@

When no encrypted cache is available, we create a fresh [[Canvas]] instance and
immediately configure it with the shared retry policy.

This contrasts the cache-hit and cache-miss paths deliberately.
Cached clients arrive with their warmed state already attached, while cache
misses must construct a fresh client and then receive the same retry behaviour
so that callers do not observe different network resilience depending on where
the object came from.

<<functions>>=
def create_canvas(hostname, token):
  """Create a new ``Canvas`` instance with shared retry configuration."""
  canvas = Canvas(hostname, token)
  return configure_canvas_retry_policy(canvas)
@

\paragraph{Declaring the urllib3 and requests we rely on.}
The [[backoff_max]] and [[backoff_jitter]] arguments exist only from
urllib3~2.0; urllib3~1.26 rejects them with a [[TypeError]].
We never got urllib3 as a declared dependency: it arrived only through
canvasapi's dependency on requests, and requests accepts urllib3~1.26.
An environment that already had an old urllib3 therefore kept it when
canvaslms was installed, and every command, [[login]] included, crashed in
[[configure_canvas_retry_policy]] before doing anything (issue \#564).
Since this module imports [[urllib3]] and [[requests]] itself (as do the
[[assignments]] and [[quizzes]] modules for [[requests]]), we declare them
ourselves in [[pyproject.toml]] with the floors this code needs: [[urllib3>=2]]
for the arguments above, and [[requests>=2.30]] because older requests pin
[[urllib3<1.27]] and would make the urllib3 floor unsatisfiable.

We verify both halves.
First, a client from [[create_canvas]] really carries the backoff settings.
Constructing a [[Canvas]] makes no request, so this exercises the real
canvasapi, requests and urllib3 objects without a network; under urllib3~1.26
it fails with the error from the issue.

<<test functions>>=
def test_retry_policy_installs_backoff_settings():
  """The shared retry policy should reach the Canvas session intact."""
  canvas = cache.create_canvas("https://canvas.example.invalid", "token")
  session = canvas._Canvas__requester._session
  for url in ["https://canvas.example.invalid", "http://canvas.example.invalid"]:
    retry = session.get_adapter(url).max_retries
    assert retry.total == 10
    assert retry.backoff_max == 256
    assert retry.backoff_jitter == 0.3
    assert 429 in retry.status_forcelist
@

Second, the installed package metadata must declare the floors, since that is
what was missing: the test above passes in any environment that happens to
have urllib3~2, as ours does.
We test the specifiers against the versions from the bug report rather than
comparing strings, so any equivalent spelling of the floor passes.
The metadata comes from the installed distribution, so after editing the
dependencies in [[pyproject.toml]] we must run [[poetry install]] before this
test sees the change.

<<test functions>>=
def test_package_declares_urllib3_and_requests_floors():
  """canvaslms must require the urllib3 and requests its retry policy needs."""
  from importlib.metadata import requires
  from packaging.requirements import Requirement

  declared = {}
  for line in requires("canvaslms"):
    requirement = Requirement(line)
    if requirement.marker is None:
      declared[requirement.name.lower()] = requirement.specifier

  assert "urllib3" in declared
  assert not declared["urllib3"].contains("1.26.5")
  assert declared["urllib3"].contains("2.0")
  assert "requests" in declared
  assert not declared["requests"].contains("2.25.1")
  assert declared["requests"].contains("2.30")
