\section{The cache command and subcommands}

The [[cache]] command provides control over the various caches.
We provide subcommands for different cache management operations:
\begin{description}
\item[[cache clear]] Clears the encrypted Canvas object cache
\item[[cache clear-attachments]] Removes old cached attachment files
\end{description}

The command's description interpolates the live TTL values (imported above) so
the help always reflects the effective windows, and it names the
[[CANVASLMS_*]] environment variables that override them.  Surfacing the
override knobs here, next to the values they control, is the discoverable place
for a user wondering why the cache feels stale or stubborn.

<<functions>>=
def add_command(subp):
  """Adds the cache command to argparse parser"""
  cache_parser = subp.add_parser(
    "cache",
    help="Manage caches",
    description=f"""
Manages the persistent caches used by canvaslms.

The Canvas object cache stores API responses (courses, assignments, users,
submissions) encrypted on disk. Objects are refreshed based on their type:

- Submissions (non-passing): {SUBMISSION_TTL_MINUTES} minutes

- The list of courses: {COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS} days

- Users: {USER_CACHE_TTL_DAYS} days

- Groups: {GROUP_CACHE_TTL_DAYS} days

- Other objects: {DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS} days

Submissions with passing grades (A, P, complete) don't expire. They are updated
when all submissions of the assignment are fetched, or by canvaslms --refresh-cache.

A course, user or assignment name that matches nothing also fetches that listing
again, unless it is younger than {MISS_REFRESH_MIN_AGE_MINUTES} minutes. If more
than {MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS} listings are older than that, or more than
{MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES} in a search over all courses (no -c, or
-c '.*'), none is fetched and a warning says so. User lookups with groups
(-G, -C) don't do this. To fetch what a command reads right away, use canvaslms
--refresh-cache; the rest of the cache is kept.

The same interval decides how soon a listing is fetched again when a read asks
for something that only someone Canvas no longer lists lacks. Raising it delays
finding someone who has returned, and 0 means every such read fetches.

These windows can be tuned via environment variables (minutes for submissions and
name misses, days for the rest): CANVASLMS_SUBMISSION_TTL_MINUTES,
CANVASLMS_COURSE_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, CANVASLMS_USER_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, CANVASLMS_GROUP_CACHE_TTL_DAYS,
CANVASLMS_QUIZ_CACHE_TTL_DAYS, CANVASLMS_DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS and
CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MINUTES. Raise them
to reuse the cache across more runs, or lower them to see fresher data sooner.

CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS and
CANVASLMS_MISS_REFRESH_MAX_LISTINGS_ALL_COURSES set the two limits on listings;
0 turns the fetching off, without a warning, for that kind of search.

The cache file is written only by a command that sent a request to Canvas. A
command answered from the cache alone leaves the file as it was, so its
modification time tells when something was last fetched.

The attachment cache stores downloaded submission files using content-addressed
storage. Files not accessed in 90 days can be cleaned with clear-attachments.
"""
  )

  cache_subp = cache_parser.add_subparsers(
    title="cache commands",
    dest="cache_command",
    required=True
  )

  <<add cache clear subcommand>>
  <<add cache clear-attachments subcommand>>
@

\subsection{The cache clear subcommand}

The [[cache clear]] subcommand removes the encrypted Canvas object cache.
This forces a fresh fetch of all Canvas data on the next run.

<<add cache clear subcommand>>=
clear_parser = cache_subp.add_parser(
  "clear",
  help="Clear the cached Canvas data"
)
clear_parser.set_defaults(func=clear_command)
@

The command used to require a configured hostname: with none, it aborted with
\enquote{No hostname configured, run [[canvaslms login]]}.
That turned away the very users who need it most.
Clearing the cache is a local file operation that never contacts Canvas, so
making it depend on credentials means a user who cleared their credentials, or
never had a working keyring, cannot clean up the files an earlier install left
behind---and is told to log in to delete files that logging in would not touch.

A missing hostname therefore selects the sweep from above instead of an error.
The hostname is not a permission to delete; it is only the piece of
information that narrows \emph{which} cache file to delete, and without it the
honest interpretation of [[cache clear]] is \enquote{clear the caches}.

We discard the token that [[load_credentials]] returns alongside the hostname.
Everywhere else in this module the token is the key that decrypts a cache
file, so binding it to a name here would suggest this command reads one---but
clearing only unlinks files, and never needs to open them.
Discarding it into [[_]] states that at the call site.

<<functions>>=
def clear_command(config, canvas, args):
  """Clears the Canvas cache"""
  import canvaslms.cli.login
  hostname, _ = canvaslms.cli.login.load_credentials(config)

  if hostname:
    <<clear the configured host's cache>>
  else:
    <<clear the caches of all hosts>>
@

Both branches print their report to standard output.
Deleting files is this command's entire effect, and it leaves nothing behind
for the user to look at afterwards, so the report \emph{is} the result---the
same role the printed listing plays for [[courses]] or [[users]].
That is the line this project draws: results go to stdout, diagnostics go to
the logger.

The reporting used to go through [[logger.info]], which put it on the wrong
side of that line.
The logger is filtered to [[WARNING]] by default, so [[canvaslms cache clear]]
printed nothing at all unless the user thought to add [[-v]]---and a command
whose only output is suppressed by default cannot be told apart from one that
silently did nothing.
The sibling [[cache clear-attachments]] already printed its report, so the two
[[cache]] subcommands disagreed about whether to say anything.

Genuine diagnostics stay with the logger: [[clear_all_caches]] still logs a
warning for each file it could not remove, because that is information about
how the sweep went rather than what the command achieved.

With a hostname we behave exactly as before.
The scheme prefix is part of the cache file name and [[login]] happily stores
a bare host name, so the hostname has to be normalized before we derive the
path.
We normalize through [[normalize_hostname]] rather than repeating the test
here: the loader ran the hostname through that same function before writing
the cache, so sharing it is what guarantees that the name we delete is the
name that was written.
<<clear the configured host's cache>>=
hostname = normalize_hostname(hostname)

if clear_cache(hostname):
  print(f"Cache cleared for {hostname}")
else:
  print(f"No cache found for {hostname}")
@

Without one we sweep, and report how many files went.
Neither branch fails when there was nothing to remove: an already-empty cache
is the state the user asked for, and scripts that clear the cache before a run
should not have to distinguish \enquote{cleared} from \enquote{already clear}.
Both outcomes are still reported, though, so that the user can tell which one
they got.
<<clear the caches of all hosts>>=
removed = clear_all_caches()

if removed:
  print(f"Cleared {removed} cache file(s) in {dirs.user_cache_dir}")
else:
  print(f"No cache files found in {dirs.user_cache_dir}")
@

Let's verify both branches, since the choice between them is the whole point
of the command's structure.
Without credentials every cache file must go; with credentials only the
configured host's file may go, even when it was configured without a scheme.

Each branch also has to say what it did, on stdout, without the user asking
for extra verbosity.
We assert that through [[capsys]] rather than [[caplog]], which is what makes
these tests notice a regression back to logger-only reporting: the default
[[WARNING]] threshold would swallow the message, and the command would go
silent again while its file-level assertions still passed.

<<test functions>>=
def test_clear_command_without_hostname_clears_all(
    tmp_cache_dir, monkeypatch, capsys):
  """Without a configured hostname, all cache files should be cleared."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
      canvaslms.cli.login,
      "load_credentials",
      lambda config: (None, None),
  )
  write_cache_file("https://one.example.com")
  write_cache_file("https://two.example.com")

  cache.clear_command({}, None, None)

  assert list(tmp_cache_dir.iterdir()) == []
  assert "2 cache file(s)" in capsys.readouterr().out

def test_clear_command_with_hostname_spares_other_hosts(
    tmp_cache_dir, monkeypatch, capsys):
  """With a configured hostname, only that host's cache should be cleared."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
      canvaslms.cli.login,
      "load_credentials",
      lambda config: ("one.example.com", "token"),
  )
  cleared = write_cache_file("https://one.example.com")
  spared = write_cache_file("https://two.example.com")

  cache.clear_command({}, None, None)

  assert not cleared.exists()
  assert spared.exists()
  assert "one.example.com" in capsys.readouterr().out

def test_clear_command_reports_when_there_was_nothing_to_clear(
    tmp_cache_dir, monkeypatch, capsys):
  """An empty cache directory should still produce a report, not silence."""
  monkeypatch.setattr(
      canvaslms.cli.login,
      "load_credentials",
      lambda config: (None, None),
  )

  cache.clear_command({}, None, None)

  assert "No cache files found" in capsys.readouterr().out
